close_range, as Linux 5.9 and glibc 2.34 have it, through a CloseRange
sysdep: it closes the open descriptors from one number to another, or
with CLOSE_RANGE_CLOEXEC sets close-on-exec on them. The function and its
two flags are declared in <unistd.h> with the glibc option and
_GNU_SOURCE, as glibc declares them. A system whose C library knows
which descriptors are open implements the sysdep, and a program then
need not list /proc/self/fd or close every number up to RLIMIT_NOFILE,
which leaves open what is above a limit that was lowered. Without the
sysdep close_range fails with ENOSYS. Meant for upstream.

diff --git a/options/glibc/generic/glibc-unistd.cpp b/options/glibc/generic/glibc-unistd.cpp
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..65aebd8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/options/glibc/generic/glibc-unistd.cpp
@@ -0,0 +1,12 @@
+#include <errno.h>
+#include <unistd.h>
+
+#include <mlibc/all-sysdeps.hpp>
+
+int close_range(unsigned int first, unsigned int last, int flags) {
+	if(int e = mlibc::sysdep_or_enosys<CloseRange>(first, last, flags); e) {
+		errno = e;
+		return -1;
+	}
+	return 0;
+}
